# Beat 9 of grpconf/synopsis.md — 2:00, the log, half-read.
#
# Payload: grpconf/stage/boblog, 20 198 bytes.
# Database: grpconf/stage/bobapp.desc — 41 files recovered from bobapp.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $BOBDESC -I $SRC \
#       --script grpconf/beats/log-partial.script $BOBLOG
#
# The document opens with NO TYPE — the truncated tail disqualifies every
# candidate (anomaly 4 is why beat 9 exists).  The heat cues take over.
#
# ENDS UNRESOLVED.  The Places payloads stay opaque bytes.  Do not resolve
# them here.  The cliffhanger is the beat.
#
# node: search strings — see infer.script's header.  After the root is
# overridden to bobapp.v1.Log, field names are: entry (field 1 repeated),
# at/method/note/request/response inside each entry.  Before the override
# the root is <raw / no type> and the fields are bare numbers.
#
# Re-pin:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $BOBDESC -I $SRC \
#       --script grpconf/beats/log-partial.script $BOBLOG script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: The log, half-read

steps:
  - text: |
      A different shape of document, and the first honest one.

      The log opened with no type at all.  Beat 8's file was named in
      50 ms; this one defeats the same machinery completely.  The cause
      is anomaly 4 — the truncated tail makes every candidate walk off
      the end of the file, and an incomplete walk is a veto.

      The heat cues still have something to say.  Press space, then
      -> to step.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: /

  - text: |
      The bracket beside this entry is a heat cue.

      protolens cannot name the document, but it can look at any piece
      of it and say: this shape is somebody's message.  It points at
      this entry and names a type, from the bytes alone, with nothing
      to go on.  Hover over the bracket, or press t to see the
      breakdown.

      Thirty-five points clear of the runner-up.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: "1 {"

  - text: |
      The command line below is prefilled.

      This override names the ENVELOPE — field 1 repeated, the log's
      own format, recovered from the same binary the descriptors came
      from.  Press Enter to apply it.

      One override, one keystroke, and the whole log opens.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: "1 {"
    override: override /1 --as bobapp.v1.Entry

  - text: |
      The log has three entries.  Unfold the first with l.

      Inside: at, method, note, request, response.  The Routes entry
      opens in the clear — ComputeRoutesRequest at -16.  Thirty-nine
      points of daylight, same as beat 8.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: entry

  - text: |
      The Places entries do not.

      Their cue is lit but dim: the best thing this database can offer
      is a type at -37.  That is the tool saying: there is a message in
      here, and I do not have it.

      Leave it hanging.  Do not resolve it here.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/1]
    node: request

  - text: |
      One more thing before we leave: the last entry.

      It does not render at all.  It comes back as one opaque run:

          #@ TRUNCATED_BYTES; MISSING: 1024

      The tool does not merely fail; it says how far short the file
      falls.  The length prefix is what makes that number knowable.

      :quit, and we reopen with a bigger dictionary.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: "TRUNCATED_BYTES"
